marble-device-support (1.0.0-16) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the modem coming up without a SIM: no PIN prompt, no registration, no
    SMS until the stack was restarted by hand. qcom-phone-utils' script
    /usr/bin/qcom-modem-setup hardcodes sim_wait_time=1 (the comment says it
    can be raised to e.g. 30), while this modem's UIM takes about ten seconds
    to report "Card state: 'present'". On every cold boot the script printed
    "E: No sim detected after 1 seconds." and exited 4, leaving the primary GW
    provisioning session inactive; ModemManager then logged "Couldn't check
    unlock status: QMI operation failed: GW primary session index unknown" and
    settled on state=failed (sim-missing). A failed modem never reports
    unlock-required=sim-pin, so Phosh had nothing to prompt with.
    - qcom-modem-setup.service.d/10-marble-sim-wait.conf points ExecStart at
      marble-modem-setup, which re-runs the stock script (from /run) with
      sim_wait_time=30.
    - marble-modem-recover.timer fires 25 s after boot and, if the modem still
      sits in failed/sim-missing, restarts qcom-modem-setup and ModemManager
      once. It is deliberately not ordered into multi-user.target, so a slow
      poll can never delay boot.
  * Depend on qcom-phone-utils (the script being wrapped) and modemmanager
    (mmcli, used by the recovery check).

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 21 Oct 2026 00:45:00 +0800

marble-device-support (1.0.0-15) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the boot-time race that left some boots without WLAN at all. The unit
    only ordered itself after local-fs.target, so on fast boots it reached the
    mount before udev had created /dev/disk/by-partlabel/persist:
    "mount: /run/marble-persist: fsconfig() failed: Can't lookup blockdev".
    set -e then killed the script before modprobe ath11k_pci, and because that
    module is blacklisted on purpose the driver never loaded: the phone came up
    with Bluetooth (bootmac handles that separately) and no wl* interface,
    i.e. no WiFi until reboot. Journal evidence: 13.2 s to connected on a good
    boot, zero ath11k lines on the bad ones.
    - Order the unit after dev-disk-by\x2dpartlabel-persist.device, mirroring
      qbootctl's wait-for-partitions.conf.
    - Resolve the partition with findfs PARTLABEL=persist (kernel property, no
      udev symlink needed) and retry for 10 s, instead of mounting the udev
      path directly.
  * Never load ath11k_pci without the factory address: a missing wlan_mac.bin
    now fails the unit instead of bringing the radio up with a random MAC.
  * Always re-copy wlan_mac.bin into the firmware directory; a stale file left
    on /lib/firmware from an earlier boot would otherwise outlive the fix.

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:50:00 +0800

marble-device-support (1.0.0-13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Require qbootctl and ship a drop-in that orders qbootctl.service after the
    boot_a and boot_b udev device units. Qualcomm ABL decrements the A/B slot
    retry counter on every boot unless the running slot is marked successful;
    without this the device eventually falls back to fastboot. The shipped
    unit only orders itself after local-fs.target, which does not guarantee
    udev has created the boot_a/boot_b symlinks.

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 18:39:16 +0800

marble-device-support (1.0.0-12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Re-arm the sensor chain deterministically after an SLPI recovery. The
    SLPI crashes once per boot (SNS_SEE fatal, ~79 s in) and remoteproc
    recreates /dev/fastrpc-sdsp right after; hexagonrpcd only sometimes
    notices. When it does not, the daemon keeps running against the pre-crash
    DSP state and every sensor reading is dead while the unit still reports
    active with zero restarts: the ambient light sensor returns a constant
    0 lux and the accelerometer stays silent. Observed on two consecutive
    boots (one self-healed, one did not - restarting hexagonrpcd by hand fixed
    it every time).
  * 82-libssc-marble-sensors.rules now wants marble-sensors-refresh.service
    when the fastrpc-sdsp node appears, and that unit try-restarts
    hexagonrpcd (a no-op when the daemon is not up yet, which is the common
    case for the boot-time event).
  * iio-sensor-proxy's drop-in gains PartOf=hexagonrpcd.service so the proxy
    restarts with the daemon instead of holding sessions it opened before the
    recovery.

